<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gastric ulcer, healing (K25.4)</content>. The six margin biopsies show a benign ulcer with no dysplasia or cancer. Symptoms have settled on vonoprazan. [S]<list><item>Continue vonoprazan 20 mg each morning to complete eight weeks.</item><item>Repeat endoscopy with biopsy of the scar at about ten weeks, because cancer cannot be fully excluded until a gastric ulcer has healed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Helicobacter pylori infection, clarithromycin-resistant (B96.81)</content>. Histology confirms H. pylori, and PCR shows a 23S rRNA mutation conferring clarithromycin resistance, so clarithromycin-based triple therapy would probably fail. Bismuth quadruple therapy is not available for this use in Japan. Vonoprazan-amoxicillin dual therapy for 14 days is the best choice: it needs no clarithromycin, and amoxicillin resistance is rare. No penicillin allergy. [S]<list><item>Vonoprazan 20 mg twice daily (an extra evening dose) with amoxicillin 1 g three times daily for 14 days, from today.</item><item>Expect loose stools or a metallic taste; call for watery diarrhea, rash or blood.</item><item>Urea breath test at least four weeks after the antibiotics and two weeks after stopping vonoprazan.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia (K29.40)</content>. The mapping biopsies show chronic active gastritis with moderate atrophy and focal intestinal metaplasia in the antrum (OLGA stage II, OLIM stage I) and no dysplasia. Eradication lowers but does not remove the risk of gastric cancer, so he needs endoscopic surveillance after eradication. [S]<list><item>Upper endoscopy yearly after eradication.</item><item>No smoking; little alcohol; less salt and pickled food.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Iron-deficiency anemia (D50.0)</content>. Hemoglobin 11.8 g/dL, up 0.8 g/dL in two weeks: responding. [S]<list><item>Continue ferrous sulfate every other morning.</item><item>Blood count in about four weeks.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Upper endoscopy with biopsy of the ulcer margin</td><td>At diagnosis; repeat for a gastric ulcer at 8 to 12 weeks to confirm healing and biopsy the scar</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Margin biopsies benign; no dysplasia or cancer; repeat endoscopy at about ten weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Helicobacter pylori test (rapid urease test and histology) with clarithromycin-resistance testing</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Histology positive; clarithromycin-resistance mutation on PCR</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Eradication therapy: adherence and side effects</td><td>At the start and during the course</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Vonoprazan-amoxicillin dual therapy for 14 days started today; adherence and side effects explained</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Test of eradication (13C urea breath test)</td><td>At least four weeks after antibiotics and two weeks after acid suppression</td><td>Due</td><td>About seven weeks after the course ends</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acid suppression for ulcer healing (vonoprazan)</td><td>Daily for eight weeks for a gastric ulcer</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taking vonoprazan each morning; pain gone</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>NSAID avoidance</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None taken</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Bleeding warning signs and when to call 119</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No tarry stools or vomiting; dark stools on iron explained</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Alcohol, smoking and meals</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Meals more regular; no smoking; less salt advised</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count on oral iron</td><td>About four weeks after starting oral iron, then every three months for a year (CMID)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hemoglobin 11.8 g/dL two weeks after starting iron, up 0.8 g/dL; next at about four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Gastric cancer surveillance: atrophy and intestinal metaplasia staging</td><td>At diagnosis, then endoscopy yearly after eradication when atrophy or intestinal metaplasia is present</td><td>Completed today</td><td>OLGA stage II, OLIM stage I; open-type atrophy; no dysplasia: yearly surveillance</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Endoscopy within three days of the first visit, with cancer excluded by biopsy. Outcome by 2026-06-04: Endoscopy three days after the first visit; margin biopsies benign at two weeks [S]</item><item>No further bleeding and no emergency admission through the episode. In progress; target 2026-08-03 [S]</item><item>Helicobacter pylori eradicated, confirmed by urea breath test. In progress; target 2026-08-03 [S]</item><item>Gastric ulcer healed at repeat endoscopy. In progress; target 2026-08-03 [S]</item><item>Hemoglobin normal and ferritin above 50 ng/mL. In progress; target 2026-08-03 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: ACG Clinical Guideline: Treatment of Helicobacter pylori Infection (2024); Management of Helicobacter pylori infection: the Maastricht VI/Florence consensus report (2022); Guidelines for the management of Helicobacter pylori infection in Japan: 2016 Revised Edition (2016 (published 2019)); Evidence-based clinical practice guidelines for peptic ulcer disease 2026 (2026); ACG Clinical Guideline: Upper Gastrointestinal and Ulcer Bleeding (2021); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, anemia monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Blood count in about four weeks. Eleven-week review in early August with the urea breath test, repeat endoscopy, blood count and ferritin. Call 119 for black, tarry stools, vomiting blood or fainting. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-16: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
